<%# app/views/shared/_attachment_strip.html.erb
    Locals: files (Array<GameFile>, e.g. Level#attached_files_for/Hint#attached_files_for),
            game  (the Game every file in `files` belongs to).

    Shared between show_current_level.html.erb's level card and each fired
    hint's fieldset -- one partial, so the two never drift.

    BELOW the caller's task text, always -- never inside it. That placement is
    the whole reason author text needs no sanitisation of its own: the design
    ("Renderer never parses author text, so the app's absolute HTML-escaping
    guarantee is untouched. No new injection surface at all.",
    docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-12-level-and-hint-attachments-design.md)
    depends on this partial never being rendered FROM WITHIN a translated()
    call. Render it as a sibling, after the text, not inside it.

    Authorization: GameFileAccess#permitted? is asked for every file before
    any tag is emitted -- its class comment says the play screen must, so
    this view never renders an <img>/<a> the delivery route would turn around
    and 404 for. A file the requester may not see is filtered out entirely,
    before the container is even considered -- see visible_files below.

    Query cost: permitted? always re-queries file_attachments (and the
    attachable it points at) for its own authorization check -- its class
    comment explains why: .includes() called on an association discards
    whatever the caller preloaded, so that part of the cost is NOT avoidable
    from here. What IS avoidable is everything else permitted? would
    otherwise ALSO query per file: it also reads file.game and, through that,
    file.game.current_run. Both are free here as long as the caller preloaded
    `file` with `:game_file => { :game => :runs }` nested under
    file_attachments -- see game_passings_controller.rb#preloaded_level and
    FileAttachable#attached_files_for's own comment on why a plain
    Level.includes(:file_attachments => ...) does not reach this method
    unless it does. DO NOT reassign `file.game` here to "help" -- doing so
    would replace the deeply-preloaded object (with :runs already loaded)
    with the `game` local, which does not carry that nested preload, turning
    a free read back into a query per file. See task-3-report.md for the
    measured query count. %>
<%
  visible_files = files.select { |file| GameFileAccess.new(current_user, file).permitted? }
%>
<% if visible_files.present? %>
  <div class="attachment-strip" aria-label="<%= t("game_passings.show_current_level.attachments_label") %>">
    <% visible_files.each do |file| %>
      <%# existing_web_variant, deliberately NOT web_variant -- the latter
          GENERATES the variant on a miss, and a render path that allocates
          disk breaks design invariant I1 (a full disk must not break the
          play screen mid-race). existing_web_variant is nil for a PDF by
          design, nil for a GIF (web_variant never resizes one, so no "web"
          derivative ever exists to read), and nil for an image whose variant
          record is missing -- all three degrade to the same generic
          indicator rather than a broken <img>. %>
      <% if file.existing_web_variant.present? %>
        <%= link_to game_file_delivery_path(game, file, "original"), :class => "attachment-item" do %>
          <%# Alt text is the filename -- author content, escaped by ERB like
              any other interpolation, never run through t(). %>
          <%= image_tag game_file_delivery_path(game, file, "web"),
                        :alt => file.filename, :class => "attachment-image" %>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <%= link_to game_file_delivery_path(game, file, "original"), :class => "attachment-item attachment-item--generic" do %>
          <span class="attachment-generic-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128206;</span>
          <span class="attachment-generic-filename"><%= file.filename %></span>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
